[x] Let sandbox sessions reach localhost =sandbox-loopback $DECIDED with Darrion 2026-10-10: the dev pod exists for agent traffic and its host loopback holds nothing sessions must be kept from; keep the LAN block. srr tests with @cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers and wrangler dev time out because IPAddressDeny=localhost blocks their own listeners in the shared host network namespace. Drop localhost and the resolver exception, document the exposure, and have doctor note loopback-reachable TCP listeners from proc fixtures. Complete when .sandbox/gate passes; the orchestrator recopies the unit and verifies the srr probe on the host. DONE 2026-10-10: unit permits loopback and retains all LAN/link-local/multicast denies; README warns about loopback and wildcard listener exposure; doctor reports address:port pairs (or none) using /proc/net/tcp and tcp6 LISTEN rows with existing awk, adding no dependency. Fixture tests cover 127/8, IPv6 loopback, wildcard listeners, excluded addresses/states, duplicates, empty and unavailable tables. sh test/doctor.test.sh, sh test/systemd.test.sh and .sandbox/gate pass. Host-only verification remains: installed unit enforcement, same-session IPv4/IPv6 and wildcard listeners, DNS, retained LAN block, and srr wrangler/vitest probe.$ ^2026-10-10T07:30 %2026-10-10T07:31 #01a1261d-0400-7000-8000-1c672bd17701
[ ] Give each session its own network namespace with pasta =sandbox-network-pasta $Revisit when a host gains loopback services worth protecting. Give sessions private loopback; pasta runs on the host per session. DNS needs pasta's forwarding and a matching resolv.conf, since 127.0.0.53 would be the session's own loopback. The network namespace lifecycle is the fiddly part.$ !5 ^2026-10-10T07:30 #01a1261d-0400-7000-8000-1c672bd17702
[ ] Route session egress through a logging proxy =sandbox-egress-proxy $Revisit when a host gains loopback services worth protecting. Use PrivateNetwork plus an HTTP proxy on the host: log every host an agent contacts and allow an allowlist. Only tools honoring HTTPS_PROXY work.$ !5 ^2026-10-10T07:30 #01a1261d-0400-7000-8000-1c672bd17703
[ ] Export sandbox telemetry with standard attributes =sandbox-telemetry $Sessions export OTLP to the NUC collector, which writes agent-logs, agent-metrics and agent-traces jsonl under ~/.local/state/otelcol. From a container, the collector answers on the tailscale address, not host.containers.internal. Attributes, standard only: service.name and service.version from the harness itself, host.name, deployment.environment.name sandbox or interactive, vcs.repository.name, vcs.ref.head.name, vcs.ref.head.revision, cicd.pipeline.name agent-sandbox, cicd.pipeline.run.id for the run and cicd.pipeline.task.run.id per session. No clearhead attributes: runs join to actions through commits. The driver also timestamps build and each session in the manifest. Write the convention, with this table and its reasons, to docs/telemetry.md; interactive harness configs adopt host.name and deployment.environment.name. Sequence after the run-kind/driver boundary and automatic reconciliation stabilize: export meaningful terminal outcomes and systemd resource events, rather than instrumenting the current mixed-policy launcher twice. 2026-09-30: this is the observability half of the review feedback loop; the other half is the review and human-verdict records in the manifests, joined by run id.  UPDATE 2026-10-09: host.containers.internal was podman-only. agent@ units allow only the resolver and the model APIs (IPAddressAllow), so reaching the collector needs an explicit allowance in the unit. UPDATE 2026-10-10: a collector on the host's loopback is now reachable without an explicit allowance.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 #01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a
[ ] Query sandbox runs with duckdb =sandbox-benchmarks $A saved duckdb query file over ~/agent-runs manifests and the collector jsonl, answering: cost and time per outcome; build time against agent time; tool calls and gate runs per session; sandbox against interactive sessions. Questions first, no dashboard. Do after sandbox-telemetry. ADDED 2026-09-30: calibration questions from the review feedback loop: per reviewer model and kind of change, the miss rate (human 'missed' entries) and false-alarm rate (overruled findings), and whether transcript coverage predicts misses.  UPDATE 2026-10-09: manifests now live in /var/lib/agent-runs (sessions/<n>.json also records the layer builds each session ran on).$ !3 ^2026-09-25T15:32 <01a0dab3-2dd5-7753-9f1d-25c1a3aea18a #01a0dab3-2df6-7862-8b3a-df005de98823
[=] Define a read-only planner and proposal handoff =sandbox-planner-contract $NEEDS DECISION: who approves and applies planner-proposed ClearHead action/charter changes—the human, a deliberating council, or a bounded driver policy? A genuinely read-only planner cannot directly mutate .clearhead; have it inspect the action, charter, specs, code and prior run evidence, validate feasibility, and emit a structured proposal with candidate CLI mutations, acceptance checks, dependencies, unknowns and stop/ask points. A distinct authorized materializer applies accepted changes through the ClearHead CLI and commits them; proposals are evidence, not authority. Alternative: grant narrowly scoped .clearhead write access in an isolated clone, but then this is a writer role, not read-only. Keep this ClearHead-specific policy in the driver, while the sandbox only hosts a generic read-only run kind and artifact. Preserve human authority over consequential decisions and avoid making a planner the top-down dispatcher. Decide the approval boundary before implementing.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:45 #01a0dc3f-bb67-76d2-a4ad-920ae1f90b3a
[=] Design interruptible interactive agent sessions =sandbox-interactive-control $NEEDS DECISION: what authenticated local input/control channel may interrupt an active sandbox model session without weakening the Git-only code-output boundary? Quadlet attachment alone cannot add a turn to current headless pi -p/Claude -p sessions. Specify cancellation versus checkpoint/resume, transport (persistent RPC or PTY relay), audit of human interventions, unit/manifest identity, credential isolation, failure/reconnect behavior, and whether a controller may write only prompts or broader tools. Preserve resource caps and independent review. This is a separate mode from unattended one-turn work; do not change the active runner until the authority and security contract is approved.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:45 #01a0dc3f-bb8e-7192-8113-f2b84efa412e
[ ] Pilot a lower-cost worker model on decided work =sandbox-cheap-worker-pilot $After the planner proposal contract and first-class cross-vendor review run exist, pilot a cheaper worker route such as DeepSeek via the sandbox harness adapter on a few bounded, already-decided actions in isolated Quadlets. Strong planning and independent review are advisory gates, not automatic authority; no self-review. Keep the same spend, wall-time and commit/gate contracts. Compare cost and elapsed time per accepted landed action, retries, stop/NEEDS DECISION rate and reviewer effort against a stronger worker baseline; cheap tokens alone are not the success metric. Record model/provider per session and let the driver select roles by policy without hard-coding ClearHead or a model into the generic sandbox. Do not make the cheap model the default for broad multi-seam or high-risk tasks without evidence.  UPDATE 2026-10-09: 'isolated Quadlets' now means agent@ sessions.$ !3 ^2026-09-25T22:46 <01a0dc3f-bb67-76d2-a4ad-920ae1f90b3a <01a0dab3-2e1a-79c2-a788-6901734ddadf #01a0dc3f-e6ce-7cd3-802a-473ab21aa73a
[x] Prove an unattended run on the homelab =sandbox-unattended $Overnight runs belong on the homelab, which stays on; the desktop is turned off and on (the human, 2026-10-03). A session is already a systemd unit; the driver that starts sessions must run under the user manager too (systemd-run --user or a user timer), not in a terminal. README, Unattended runs, says what the host needs; agent-doctor checks lingering and whether the user manager's PATH finds agent-run. Host side, handed to the personal-os agent: lingering for the account, an environment.d PATH entry from dotfiles (the line in the README was checked with systemd's environment-d generator), and no suspend. Proof: on the homelab, a driver started with systemd-run --user runs two sessions while nobody is logged in, and agent-result shows both finished afterwards. Which queue runs overnight and on what schedule is the project driver's decision (platform's clearhead-work), not the sandbox's. BLOCKED 2026-10-09: the NUC must first run personal-os as the dev pod (meta-analysis DECISIONS 03acf7c; personal-os action 01a11dc8). The facilitator will live there as root, so the driver may run under root's manager rather than a user's. DONE 2026-10-10 on devbox (facilitator session 10), as agent-facilitator, not root (Claude Code refuses bypass mode as root). Driver = a user unit run by lingering at boot (WantedBy=default.target), ~16 s after power-on; loginctl at its start listed only the user manager's own manager-class session, nobody logged in. It ran a claude then a pi read-only session; agent-result showed both finished. Same pair also passed from systemd-run --user before the reboot. Chain covered: TPM-unlocked /home, environment.d PATH in the user manager, credstore.encrypted Claude token, shared pi login. Gap: the facilitator is in wheel and the dev-pod polkit rule grants wheel everything, so this did not prove the agents rule alone.$ !2 ^2026-10-03T11:39 %2026-10-10T06:33 #01a10310-b607-70b0-88fb-1ce5c78dfa5b
[x] Document creating the shared pi login for the systemd runtime $The documented command (podman run --rm -it --userns=keep-id -v agent-pi:/home/agent/.pi/agent localhost/<repo>-agent pi, then /login) fails at the last step for the openai-codex provider. The browser's OAuth redirect to localhost reaches the host, not the container, which publishes no ports. Adding --network=host worked on 2026-10-08. Alternative: publish only the callback port, if pi binds it on all interfaces.  UPDATE 2026-10-09: the podman command is gone from the README, but nothing says how to create the shared pi login at /var/lib/agent-runs/.pi (mounted at /srv/pi-login). Remaining: document that step for the systemd runtime, including the OAuth redirect to localhost. DONE 2026-10-10: README documents host PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR=/var/lib/agent-runs/.pi pi then /login, the direct localhost redirect, matching AGENT_PI_PROVIDER, root:agents 2770 directory and group-readable/writable auth.json for session refresh. .sandbox/gate passes. Actual host login/renewal, mounts and refresh permissions remain for the orchestrator to verify.$ +bug ^2026-10-08T10:05 %2026-10-10T03:12 #01a11c7a-1513-707f-ae0b-9c1242f0c798
[x] Make the pi provider configurable and check its login fits $DECIDED 2026-10-08 (Darrion): take the recommendation below, a provider setting plus a doctor check that its login exists; no injected host auth.json. Context: agents/session hard-coded --provider openai-codex, which pi 0.99.2 labels legacy; /login stored the token under openai, so the session failed at \$0 (run 20261008-100519). Fixed in 3fc7394 by hard-coding openai instead, so the next rename breaks it again. agent-doctor passed throughout because it only checks that auth.json is non-empty. Recommendation: AGENT_PI_PROVIDER in agents/models.env next to AGENT_PI_MODEL (same override rules), passed to --provider, and agent-doctor checks that auth.json has a key for that provider. Fold in the README login bug (--network=host) at the same time. Considered 2026-10-08: inject the host's auth.json, since pi holds several logins. Against it: OAuth refresh tokens rotate, so a shared login means whichever side refreshes first logs the other out, and a read-only mount can't refresh at all. It would also hand every provider's credentials to every session (the README already says never the host's auth.json). The agent-pi volume can hold several logins too; what is missing is choosing the provider per session and checking that its login exists. API-key providers could use one podman secret each, like claude_token. DONE 2026-10-10: AGENT_PI_PROVIDER defaults to openai in installed models.env, honors the launching environment, travels through run.env alongside the model, and reaches pi --provider. Doctor uses read-only jq to check the selected provider key in the shared auth.json and names it in success/failure output; no host pi dependency or token refresh. Stub tests cover launch transport, provider arguments, matching/mismatched logins, missing and malformed files; .sandbox/gate passes. A real host pi session remains the orchestrator's verification.$ ^2026-10-08T10:10 %2026-10-10T03:12 #01a11c7e-7882-705f-aa01-05b735f9a6b2
[ ] Retire a workspace that produced no work $A failed or empty run (e.g. 20261008-100519: startup failure, no commits) has no way out except landing. Proposed: agent-retire <workspace> refuses if a session is running or any repo has commits beyond refs/agent/base, then removes the clone and any candidate and keeps sessions, prompts and transcripts, like a successful landing does. It records the retirement in the manifest so agent-status stops listing it as open.$ ^2026-10-08T10:10 #01a11c7e-7895-7410-b077-9da607f8962e
[ ] Let sessions raise a hand\: question files, agent-answer, and waiting questions in agent-status =raise-hand $Idea (Darrion and the assistant, 2026-10-08); not scheduled. Today a stuck session's only hand is the stop rule: blocked plus NEEDS DECISION, then a restart. Decided 2026-10-08 (Darrion): this is a sandbox feature, and no separate inbox tool. Shape: (1) a session writes a question file into its workspace dir (/job/<ws>/questions/<n>.md, outside work/, so git output stays clean) and blocks on one helper call that waits for an answer file or a timeout. On timeout it falls back to the normal NEEDS DECISION stop, so a question that outlives the session lands in the project's own task system, not here. One call, not a polling loop, so waiting costs no tokens. (2) agent-status shows waiting questions: a count per workspace and the question's first line, plus a quick check for 'anything to answer?' (exits non-zero when any are waiting). (3) agent-answer <ws>/<q> <text|file> writes the answer. Who answers: whoever launched the session. An orchestrator answers context questions and escalates design ones to the human. Narrower than sandbox-interactive-control: the agent initiates, plain files, nothing new can get in except text the agent reads. Costs to weigh: a waiting session holds its container, writer lock and deadline clock, and cheap asking can replace deciding (a question mid-task often means the action was under-decided). Evidence to watch first: stops whose answer was one line. UPDATE 2026-10-10 (Darrion and the assistant, scheduled): one of the two coordination pieces needed now (the other is =claim-guard). Add a host-side wait: agent-status --wait-question (or similar) blocks until any workspace has a waiting question and then exits, so the facilitator runs it as a background task and is woken by the question instead of polling. A question is data the answerer reads, never keystrokes into the answerer: no session ever gets a channel into the facilitator terminal.$ !3 ^2026-10-08T10:21 #01a11c88-c566-7139-be11-4b8486f9c373
[ ] Evaluate VM isolation with systemd-vmspawn instead of agent\@ units $Question, not scheduled (Darrion and the assistant, 2026-10-08). Agents run arbitrary code; rootless containers share the host kernel and a VM does not. That matters most on a host where the orchestrator runs as root, where mkosi and vmspawn are the native tools. Cost of switching: replacing what Podman provides today (rootless keep-id so commits stay the user's, Quadlet units, named cache volumes, secrets) and changing the project contract from a root Containerfile to an mkosi config in every repository. Earlier attempt: a cwork script using sudo systemd-nspawn; needing root is probably why Podman won. No failure so far has come from Podman. First, a cheap probe: can an unprivileged systemd-vmspawn boot a session image in seconds and share /job/<ws> with the guest (virtiofs)? If not, the question is moot. Revisit when the sandbox moves to a root-orchestrator host.  UPDATE 2026-10-09: Podman is gone; the comparison is now agent@ units (namespaces on the host kernel) against systemd-vmspawn (own kernel). The question stands.$ !6 ^2026-10-08T10:31 #01a11c91-d731-7001-ac3a-4f6c93ecd401
[ ] Don't reuse a half-built landing candidate $build_candidate returns early when the candidate dir exists (\[ -d "\$cand" ] && return 0), but it can stop partway through: a submodule on a detached HEAD stops it after clone_local, leaving later repos without their branch switch or refs/agent/land-base. The next agent-land reuses that partial candidate, merges into it, runs the gate on a stale tree (it passed), then advance() fails with 'fatal: ambiguous argument refs/agent/land-base' and blames the repo with 'moved since the candidate was built'. Seen 2026-10-08 on workspace 20261008-100832. Fix: build into a temp dir and rename it into place only when every repo is marked, or remove the candidate on any stop inside build_candidate. Also run the detached-HEAD check on every repo before cloning anything. Second, related trap (same day): the candidate starts each submodule at the root repo's pinned commit (switch -C <branch> resets the branch name there) and records that as land-base. So a real submodule branch that is ahead of the pin can never advance, and the message says it 'moved since the candidate was built' when it never matched. Either check 'real branch == pinned commit' up front, with a message saying to bump the pointer first, or start the candidate from each real branch's tip.$ !3 +bug ^2026-10-08T10:52 #01a11ca5-8ac6-75e1-a249-607283abe5d2
[ ] Find why pi sessions block subagents; allow them if spend stays visible $agents/session runs pi with --exclude-tools bg_run,bg_delegate,subagent; the reason isn't recorded (carried over from agents/loop in adc89ea). Claude sessions allow subagents and cap spend with --max-budget-usd. Likely reasons: background tools outlive a headless session, and pi's cap is computed from message_end usage events that may not include subagent costs. Check whether subagent usage reaches the session's JSON log; if it does, allow subagent (keep bg_* blocked) so workers can save tokens the way the orchestrator does. Sessions must never launch sandbox sessions themselves (agent-run from inside); that stays with an orchestrator on the host (2026-10-08, Darrion and the assistant).$ !5 ^2026-10-08T11:30 #01a11cc7-d14a-75c1-afca-968ade66ddbd
[ ] Clean up tools trees of old base snapshots $agent-layer keeps one mkosi tools tree per base snapshot at \$AGENT_LAYERS/.tools/<SYSEXT_LEVEL> (~1.3 GB each) and never removes old ones. When the base moves to a new snapshot, the old tree is dead weight. Do it when the base first moves (nothing to clean before that): remove trees whose level no layer record references, under the build locks. Files are read-only (chmod -R u+w before rm).$ !5 ^2026-10-09T16:13 #01a122f1-9328-71da-815a-9de4658c7523
[x] Give agent-layer's mkosi an explicit package cache $Found 2026-10-10 on devbox: agent-layer runs mkosi under unshare -r, so mkosi sees uid 0 and defaults its package cache to /var/cache/mkosi (mkosi/user.py: is_regular_user is uid >= 1000). Unwritable in the namespace: Permission denied. The desktop works only because a root-owned /var/cache/mkosi/arch~rolling~x86-64/cache from April exists. Devbox works around it with XDG_CACHE_HOME in the user's environment.d. Fix: pass --package-cache-directory explicitly, and test without /var/cache/mkosi. DONE 2026-10-10: agent-layer creates and passes \$AGENT_LAYERS/.cache/packages/<name>, retained across build cleanup and serialized by the per-layer build lock; no extra host declaration or XDG_CACHE_HOME dependency. Stubbed mkosi checks the exact flag and a pre-existing writable directory, writes a probe, and tests run with XDG_CACHE_HOME and CACHE_DIRECTORY unset. sh test/layer.test.sh and .sandbox/gate pass. The orchestrator must verify a real host build without XDG_CACHE_HOME.$ +bug ^2026-10-09T22:52 %2026-10-10T06:36 #01a1245e-7f05-70e8-ac58-63f957e18483
[x] Have agent-doctor note plain and TPM-sealed credentials $Found 2026-10-10 on devbox: the Claude token is sealed with systemd-creds in /etc/credstore.encrypted/agent.claude_token, which ImportCredential=agent.* reads (verified: a session authenticated with it). Both /etc/credstore and /etc/credstore.encrypted are root:root 0700, so the doctor cannot see either directory as the calling user. Note both locations without attempting privileged detection; keep the ok path for a readable plain file. README Host setup should mention credstore.encrypted as the TPM-sealed option. DONE 2026-10-10: doctor notes both paths, plain versus systemd-creds encrypt sealing (e.g. TPM), and the inability to check either root-only store as this user. README documents the sealed alternative; the existing ImportCredential=agent.* unit is unchanged. test/doctor.test.sh asserts the full note; sh test/doctor.test.sh and .sandbox/gate pass. Host credential import and authentication remain the orchestrator's verification.$ ^2026-10-09T22:52 %2026-10-10T06:36 #01a1245e-7f1d-7503-a3bc-6182a209b72b
[x] agent-land refuses a landing that changed a Tests\: commit's files =tests-lock $From the assistant session 13 tests-first trials (srr =browse-filters, =shops-are-yards): one agent writes tests and commits them as "Tests: ..."; another implements and must not change those files. The facilitator checked this by hand each time (git diff --stat of the tests commit to HEAD over its files is empty). Mechanical, so it should cost 0 tokens: agent-land finds "Tests:" commits on the branch and refuses if a later non-"Tests:" commit changes their files. Related: run kinds for tests-first (tests, then work against tests) and a review sandbox that can run tests (all 4 Claude reviews that day hit EROFS on node_modules/.vite-temp). DONE 2026-10-10: agent-land checks harvested branch history in every repo before candidate merges, using Git and POSIX sh only. Descendant non-Tests: commits touching locked paths are refused with repository, file and both commit ids; later Tests: commits remain allowed and do not erase breaches. Tests cover respected locks, multi-file edits restored later, submodule refusal before any merge/gate, renames with newline paths and exact subject prefixes. README documents the rule and history-repair retry (no override). sh test/land.test.sh and .sandbox/gate pass. Real host landing, image mounts, isolation and systemd lifecycle remain for the orchestrator; no agents/ changes.$ !2 ^2026-10-10T19:36 %2026-10-10T23:09 #01a12750-d13c-7ec3-bd30-4bc608bf1c58
[ ] Interactive sessions through tmux =interactive $Darrion, 2026-10-10: talk to a sandboxed agent mid-run, and watch it together. agent-run --interactive sets AGENT_SESSION=interactive; agents/session then runs the harness TUI under tmux in the foreground (tmux -D, fits Type=exec) with its socket at /srv/job/WS/tmux.sock, i.e. /var/lib/agent-runs/WS/tmux.sock on the host. server-access lists exactly the facilitator and Darrion (the server runs as agent; tmux refuses other users by default). pipe-pane logs the whole session into transcripts/. Host side: tmux -S SOCK attach for a person; send-keys, capture-pane -S - and control mode for the facilitator. Same unit, isolation, limits, harvest and land. Needs tmux in layers/harness. Chose tmux over a headless nvim server (also probed): pipe-pane transcripts, scrollback capture and control mode, smaller surface in the sandbox. For mid-run questions, the harness resume modes (pi --session, codex exec resume, claude -p --resume) stay the cheaper path; this is for steering and watching. UPDATE 2026-10-10: workers never get a channel into the facilitator terminal: the facilitator holds each worker socket, never the reverse; a worker reaches the facilitator only through =raise-hand question files. Parked behind =raise-hand and =claim-guard: build when the assistant log shows \[steer] or \[relay] needs, or for Darrion watching.$ !3 ^2026-10-10T21:09 #01a127a6-1e1c-7b93-a352-df5b9a124132
[ ] Inventory surfaces shared between sandbox runs =shared-surfaces $From the METR report on the OpenAI and Hugging Face incident (2026-08-26): about 1,200 eval agents turned a shared writable package cache into an unsanctioned message board, treated other agents requests as instructions, and tampered with their own transcripts. Inventory every path one run can write and another run (or the host) later reads, and decide each one: today /srv/pi-login (the shared pi login, rw for every run), /srv/cache (per repo, rw across workspaces), and transcripts/ (written inside the run, so the agent can edit its own record). Transcripts should be written by something outside the sandbox, or at least checksummed from outside when the session ends. Coordination between agents should go through sanctioned, visible channels (ClearHead, git, =interactive), never through a side effect.$ !3 ^2026-10-10T21:13 #01a127aa-39eb-7f90-bbd0-fdabbd30c0e5
[x] Refuse host edits to a project a session holds =claim-guard $Darrion and the assistant, 2026-10-10: nobody, facilitator included, edits a project tracked files on the host while a sandbox session holds that project, because the landing merge then conflicts. The facilitator did it three times in one day (session 13, srr next.actions) despite a logged rule, so it becomes a tool. Shape to decide: a check (agent-status --held REPO, exits non-zero while a session of that repo is running) plus a pre-commit hook installed into the host clone that refuses a commit while the repo is held, with an override for the human. Weigh merge=union for .actions files as a cheaper partial fix: it resolves appends but can garble a line both sides changed. DONE 2026-10-10: facilitator clarified held exits 0 (1 otherwise), explicit host hook installation, human override only via git commit --no-verify, and no merge=union. agent-status --held resolves host checkout paths, prints matching workspaces and checks running records against unit state (readers included, manager silence retains holds). agent-doctor --install-hooks honors core.hooksPath and refuses existing different hooks; the pre-commit guard names holds and refuses check errors. README documents opt-in, PATH and commit-time limitations. Stubbed held/not-held and real Git hook tests pass, including missing runs, no sessions, multiple holders, stale records, aliases, override and existing hooks; .sandbox/gate passes. Orchestrator must verify installation and refusal/release against a real host session; no agents or unit changes.$ !3 ^2026-10-10T22:19 %2026-10-10T23:11 #01a127e6-12f1-7930-b6e0-dc24a97ca6a5
[ ] Lock tests by session, not by commit subject =tests-lock-by-session $From reading =tests-lock (aa32958): the lock trusts commit subjects, so an implementer that titles its commit "Tests: ..." can change locked files. Lock by session instead: files touched by the tests session (recorded per session in the manifest commits) may not change in any later session, whatever the subject. Also: a merge of master into the branch shows upstream changes to locked files under -m and is refused; acceptable (fails safe) but say so in the refusal.$ !3 ^2026-10-10T23:11 #01a12815-909a-77e2-a705-379ec564d66c
[x] Make the claim guard fail closed and upgradable =claim-guard-hardening $Second read of =claim-guard (Copilot claude-sonnet-5.5 review, 20261010-233544/1): (1) running_sessions swallows a jq parse error on a corrupt session record, so the repo reads as not held and the hook allows the commit: fail closed (exit 2). (2) agent-status exits 1 on any unexpected set -e abort, which the hook reads as "not held": give "not held" a distinct, explicit code or check for a clean run. (3) Installed hooks can never be upgraded (byte-identical check): install a stub that execs this checkout lib/agent-pre-commit.sh, so updates apply. (4) Print the unit state next to a holding workspace so a stale hold is visible. Relative manifest repo paths and linked worktrees: note in README. DONE 2026-10-10: session parsing propagates errors (including empty and invalid records); only explicit exit 3 means not held, so unexpected exit 1 refuses commits. Doctor installs a quoted forwarding stub and upgrades old byte-copied guards by their marker, without replacing unmarked human hooks or symlinks. Holds show tab-separated unit state; failed or silent queries retain them as unavailable. README documents absolute manifest paths and distinct linked-worktree holds. Regression tests cover all four points, live implementation updates, missing implementation and failed unit queries. sh test/claim-guard.test.sh, sh test/runs.test.sh, sh test/systemd.test.sh, sh test/doctor.test.sh and .sandbox/gate pass. Real host installation/upgrade and refusal/release against a live session remain for the orchestrator; no agents or unit changes.$ !3 ^2026-10-10T23:37 %2026-10-10T23:40 #01a1282d-8552-78e1-87ae-7dcdddf45748
[ ] Harvest warns about commits outside session bundles =harvest-stray-commits $Session 13 (2026-10-10): the facilitator committed a worker fix by hand in workspace 20261010-230752 after its session ended; agent-harvest fetches only the bundles each session exports, so the commit was silently dropped and master kept the bugs it fixed. agent-harvest should refuse (or loudly warn) when the workspace branch HEAD has commits outside every session bundle, naming them, so nothing is lost without a word.$ !3 ^2026-10-10T23:43 #01a12833-599b-7103-95f6-1c3dcce6488b
